# ── api (every ConfigSchema key; apps/api/test/env-example.test.ts fails if one is missing) ──
NODE_ENV=production
PORT=3000
DATABASE_URL=postgres://kb:change-me@db:5432/kb
# Required in production. Generate it — `openssl rand -hex 32`. The production guard refuses a
# *class* of values, not one string: the API's dev default, anything containing change / example /
# secret / placeholder / dev / test, fewer than 32 characters, or a low-diversity string — so a
# template that was copied and never filled in fails at INSTALL.md step 5 instead of signing
# session cookies with a value anyone can read in this repository.
SESSION_SECRET=change-me
PUBLIC_URL=https://kb.wecom.local
# Required in production — the API refuses to start without it. req.ip behind the nginx
# reverse proxy: true | false | comma-separated trusted proxy addresses/CIDRs. Keep it as
# the docker bridge CIDR so a client that reaches the API directly cannot forge
# X-Forwarded-For. req.ip gates PALOALTO_SUBNETS, the auth rate limits and the audit log.
TRUST_PROXY=172.16.0.0/12
# How far out to unwind X-Forwarded-For, at most. `1` = "believe exactly the address nginx
# wrote", which is the shipped topology (nginx is the edge and replaces the header). Raise it
# only if you put another proxy in front, and by the number of proxies you added; leaving it
# unset unwinds the whole trusted chain, which is what let an appending proxy pass a client's
# own X-Forwarded-For through to req.ip.
TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=1

# Never set these in a deployment. `deploy/e2e.env` marks itself with WECOM_E2E_STACK=1 so a
# deploy/.env left behind by a killed `pnpm e2e:compose` run is recognisable; the API refuses to
# boot in production with it unless WECOM_E2E_RUNNER=1, which only the e2e runner sets.
# WECOM_E2E_STACK=
# WECOM_E2E_RUNNER=
# Local (break-glass) sign-ins per minute per IP; default 5. Production may lower it but the API
# refuses to boot with a higher value unless WECOM_E2E_RUNNER=1 — it exists for the e2e gate.
# AUTH_LOCAL_RATE_LIMIT=5

# ── identity ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
OIDC_ISSUER=https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=https://kb.wecom.local/api/v1/auth/callback
# Microsoft Graph base URL — leave blank for the global cloud. Set it only for a national cloud
# (e.g. https://graph.microsoft.us). Used by the nightly identity sync and the admin group search.
# OIDC_GRAPH_URL=
AUTH_FALLBACK=none
PALOALTO_HOST=
PALOALTO_API_KEY=
PALOALTO_SUBNETS=10.0.0.0/8
# https in production; http only exists so the test suite can run a local stub firewall.
PALOALTO_SCHEME=https

# ── model (CPU-only inference) ───────────────────────────────────────────
MODEL_URL=http://ollama:11434
# The generation tag used when no tier and no slot override is set. With MODEL_TIER below it is
# the fallback only, not what the stack runs.
MODEL_NAME=qwen2.5:3b-instruct-q4_K_M
# Wave 6: the multilingual embedder that comes with tier 1 (see MODEL_TIER below). Stated
# explicitly, together with EMBED_DIMENSION, because migration 0051 builds
# `documents.embedding` at exactly the width these two resolve to.
EMBED_MODEL=bge-m3
# How many dimensions EMBED_MODEL returns. It must equal the width of `documents.embedding` —
# and the API reads that column's own declared width at start-up and refuses to boot on a
# disagreement, naming both numbers.
#
# Wave 6: the column is no longer a number typed into a migration. 0003 created `vector(768)`;
# 0051 rebuilds it at whatever `EMBED_DIMENSION`/`MODEL_TIER` resolve to, with the *same*
# precedence the boot check uses (explicit value → tier preset → 768). So this line and the
# migration cannot disagree: whichever width you configure is the width the column gets.
#
# This exists because the mismatch used to be invisible. `updateEmbedding` is best-effort and
# swallows its errors so a model outage can never fail a publish, so an EMBED_MODEL of the wrong
# width (`all-minilm` is 384) stored no vector on any publish, logged nothing, and left search
# ranking lexically for ever — with a green `/system/health` throughout.
#
# Changing the embedding model is therefore a two-line change, and both lines must agree:
#   bge-m3            1024  (what this file ships, with MODEL_TIER=1)
#   nomic-embed-text  768   (the pre-wave-6 default; deploy/ci.env and deploy/e2e.env keep it)
#   mxbai-embed-large 1024
#   all-minilm        384
# A different width also needs the column rebuilt to match (re-run 0051) and every document
# re-embedded (`POST /admin/ai/reindex`, queue `ai.reindex`) — existing vectors are not
# convertible, and search ranks lexically until that job finishes.
# `/system/health`'s `embedStatus` reports the model, the width it last returned, and this number.
EMBED_DIMENSION=1024
# true | false (only these two spellings; anything else is a start-up error).
# true falls back to the deterministic rule-based model — no Ollama call at all.
MODEL_DISABLED=false
# ── wave 6: model tier and the two generation slots ──────────────────────
# MODEL_TIER selects a whole row of the tier table (spec §6 / docs/api/CONTRACTS-wave6.md):
# the suggestion model, the chat model, the embedder and its width, in one number.
#   0  qwen2.5:3b        + nomic-embed-text (768)   today's baseline, kept for comparison
#   1  DictaLM 2.0 7B q4 + bge-m3 (1024)            4 vCPU / 16 GB — the wave 6 default
#   2  Gemma 3 12B q4    + bge-m3                   8 vCPU / 32 GB
#   3  Gemma 3 27B q4    + bge-m3                  16 vCPU / 64 GB
#   4  27–32B q4         + bge-m3                  + GPU 24 GB VRAM — interactive chat
# Unset means nothing changes from the pre-wave-6 behaviour: both slots use MODEL_NAME and the
# embedder stays whatever EMBED_MODEL/EMBED_DIMENSION say. A *fresh* install ships at tier 1,
# below, because that is the configuration wave 6 was evaluated on (Hebrew-specialised
# generation, a multilingual embedder); an existing install stays where it is until an operator
# changes this line, the two lines above it and re-runs 0051 together.
#
# Selecting a tier whose embedder differs from the current one is NOT a one-line change: the
# `documents.embedding` column has to be rebuilt at the new width (migrate down to 0050 and up
# again, in a maintenance window) and every document re-embedded (`POST /admin/ai/reindex`) —
# see EMBED_DIMENSION above and docs/operations.md § Model tiers.
#
# `deploy/ollama-pull.sh` resolves this tier exactly as the API does, so a clean install pulls
# the tags the API will actually ask for. `POST /admin/ai/models/test` proves a tag exists
# before an admin commits to a tier.
MODEL_TIER=1
# Per-slot overrides. Each beats the tier preset; empty means "use the tier, else MODEL_NAME".
# Tier 1's generation tag is aya-expanse:8b-q4_K_M; its fallback is qwen2.5:7b-instruct-q4_K_M.
# (DictaLM 2.0 has no Ollama-library tag — see docs/wave6-acceptance.md § Model evaluation.)
# SUGGEST_MODEL=aya-expanse:8b-q4_K_M
# CHAT_MODEL=aya-expanse:8b-q4_K_M

# ── sources / connectors ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Watched folder: every settled .docx in it is imported as a source every 2 minutes.
# Mounted as the `watch` volume on the api service; leave empty to disable.
WATCH_DIR=/data/watch
# Required in production — the all-zero default is rejected at start-up. AES-256-GCM key
# for connector configs at rest (WordPress application password, webhook secret).
# Generate once with `openssl rand -hex 32` and keep it with the database backups:
# rotating it makes existing connectors unreadable.
CONNECTOR_KEY=
# A `json` connector may only read files under this directory.
CONNECTOR_FILE_ROOT=/data/connectors
# Required in production — the API refuses to start with this empty. Comma-separated host
# allowlist for outbound connector HTTP (a WordPress baseUrl). Name the connector hosts you
# actually use, e.g. `wp.wecom.local,.wecom.local`; an entry starting with `.` matches any
# subdomain. Link-local/metadata addresses are refused unconditionally, everything else is
# governed by this list — it is what stops a connector becoming an SSRF primitive.
# `*` means "any PUBLIC host": loopback, private (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16), carrier-grade
# NAT and IPv6 unique-local addresses stay refused under it, so `*` cannot reach the model on
# 127.0.0.1:11434 or the database port. A named entry always wins — list `127.0.0.1` or your
# LAN host if a connector really must reach one. An empty value is unrestricted, which is
# exactly why production refuses to start with it.
CONNECTOR_HOST_ALLOWLIST=
# Whether an incoming webhook must carry the `X-KB-Nonce` header (see docs/connectors.md).
# Replay protection does not depend on this — the API remembers a hash of every signed body it
# accepts and answers 409 REPLAY to the second delivery either way. Leave it false for one
# release while WordPress plugins older than the header are still installed; each such delivery
# logs a deprecation warning. Set it true once every site is updated, so a stale plugin fails
# loudly rather than quietly.
WEBHOOK_REQUIRE_NONCE=false

# ── retention ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
BACKUP_DIR=/backups
TRASH_DAYS=30

# ── compose only ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me
TLS_CERT_PATH=./certs/cert.pem
TLS_KEY_PATH=./certs/key.pem
BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=14
WEB_HTTPS_PORT=443
WEB_HTTP_PORT=80
MIGRATE_ON_START=true
